README -- what was changed in tet, and whyThe ARCHIVE build of tet drew its board and never saw a keystroke.  Thissource has the three faults fixed, and CMDS/GAMES/tet IS this source, builtby the recipe in tools/rebuild/recipes.psv -- installed 2026-08-27 afterrdoggett play-tested the archive build at a real terminal and it took nokeys.  The archive binary is in git history.THREE FAULTS, STACKED.  Each one hid the next.1. THE KEYBOARD CODE WAS NOT COMPILED IN.  tet.c sets raw mode and reopens   stdin inside `#ifndef OSK'.  The OS-9 build got the #else branch, which is   `srand(0);' and nothing else -- no terminal setup at all.  Building with   that path enabled is the first step, and it needs LIB/unix.l, whose   ioctl.c implements TCGETA/TCSETA over _ss_opt.2. LIB/unix.l's ioctl HAD NO TCSETAW CASE.  tet goes raw with   `ioctl(1, TCSETAW, &tty)', and unixlib implemented TCSETA but not TCSETAW.   The call fell through silently, so the terminal kept echoing and the   program saw nothing.  SRC/unixlib/ioctl.c now sends TCSETAW and TCSETAF   into the TCSETA case -- OS-9's _ss_opt takes effect at once, so the   "after output drains" distinction does not arise.  LIB/unix.l was rebuilt.   That fix is not tet's alone: any program here setting terminal modes the   usual way was hitting the same silent no-op.3. IT REOPENED STDIN WITH NO ACCESS.  This is the one that actually stops the   reads:       close (0);       open (ttnam, O_NDELAY);   O_NDELAY is a Unix open() FLAG.  OS-9's open() takes an ACCESS MODE, and   DEFS/os9lib/fcntl.h defines O_NDELAY as 0 -- no read, no write.  So tet   closed its own input and reopened it unreadable.  Writes on fd 1 still   worked, which is exactly why the board draws and no key ever arrives.   Changed to S_IREAD.AND ONE CONSEQUENCE OF FIXING 3.  GetKey() loops `while read(0,...) != 0',which needs a read that returns 0 when nothing is waiting.  That is whatO_NDELAY was for on Unix, and OS-9 has no equivalent flag on a path -- ablocking read would hang the game between keystrokes and the piece wouldnever fall.  OS-9 asks first instead, so GetKey now calls _gs_rdy(0) for thecount of characters waiting and returns when it is zero.ALSO NEEDED.  randint.c, a two-line shim.  tet's makefile links/dd/lib/rand.r for randint(), and that object is on no disk here and in noarchive in the pool; the name survives only inside a comment inSRC/shuffle/xrand.c, whose own function is called rnd_ri.  Same file astools/rebuild/shims/os9randint.c.A FOURTH THING, once it took keys at all: IT PLAYED FAR TOO FAST.  The fall rate was never a clock.  A piece drops one cell when this loop  runs out:      Pause = CurrentPause;      /* 150 */      while (Pause) {              Pause--;              ...act on the last key...              GetKey();      }  150 polling passes, each as long as the machine takes.  On a 1990 68000  that came to about a second a cell.  On anything quicker it is instant,  and the game is unplayable -- the archive's own README hints at this,  naming a compile-time INIT_PAUSE for machine speed.  Each pass now sleeps one system tick, `tsleep(PAUSE_TICKS)'.  At  os9exec's default 100Hz that is 10ms, so INIT_PAUSE of 150 gives about  1.5 seconds a cell at the start, and the game speeds up as it always did  (CurrentPause-- on each completed row).  The point is that the rate is  now REAL TIME and does not change with the speed of the machine.  Both numbers are #defines at the top of tet.c.  Raise INIT_PAUSE for a  slower game; raise PAUSE_TICKS instead if your system tick is faster  than 100Hz.  Keys stay responsive either way, because the loop still  polls every pass -- it just waits a tick between polls.BUILDING    cc tet.c MoveR.c MoveL.c NewP.c AdvanceP.c Rotate.c randint.c \       -qm=16k -n=tet -f=R_tet \       -l=/dd/LIB/unix.l -l=/dd/LIB/curses.l -l=/dd/LIB/termlib.l \       -l=/dd/LIB/math.l  Note there is no -DOSK: the point is to compile the non-OSK branch.